The test is creating two `Vector3D` instances `u1` and `u2` with very large initial values which might be causing numerical precision issues during the rotation creation.

Inspecting the `Vector3D` constructor code snippet:
```java
public Vector3D(double x, double y, double z) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
    this.z = z;
}
```

The `Vector3D` constructor simply assigns the input values to the corresponding fields `x`, `y`, and `z`. The very large values used in the test may be causing precision errors during the rotation calculation, leading to NaN results.

To fix this issue, it may be necessary to handle large numbers more carefully in the rotation calculation logic.

